Renders the frozen documents in docs/ (written by the build before the simplification) through the real engine in Chromium: `npm run harness -- --out run.json`.
Hashes of text-bearing renders depend on the installed fonts, so compare a commit against its parent on ONE machine (`git worktree add`, then `node scripts/harness/compare.mjs parent.json commit.json`); only text-free hashes are committed (text-free.sha256.json).
From the commit that adds src/state/normalize.ts, each document is loaded through it exactly as an old save would be.
Besides the frozen documents, `default`, `backing-photo`, `small-2` and `small-4` are built live from the build under test (the default print, over the test photo, and at 20% / 40% type), and four that reach the corners of the press: `grain-photo` (grain over the photo, both plates with a second ink), `backing-second-ink` (a backing beside a second ink at its own switch point, turned), `press-max` (grain at screen 36, misregistration 30, edge 1, wear 0.8, type at 0.16 of the height) and `native-photo` (a 2400x3000 photo on 4:5: at height 3000 the export is the photo's own pixels). All are 4:5, so every height recorded (300, 1000, 1800) is one tile of the band engine's glyph grid; a document of another shape needs its own check of that.
`--time doc@h` renders through the export's entry point (`renderPrintAsync`, no cache), so a timing is of what Save runs.
`--scale doc@h,...` renders each document at h and 2h and prints corr(box2(render(2h)), render(h)): 1 is the same print at both sizes.
`fidelity.html` / `fidelity.ts` is the page scripts/measure-fidelity.mjs drives: the real import (into this page's IndexedDB), the real decode for Save and the real `savePrint`, on generated zone plates, an EXIF-rotated JPEG and a PNG with a transparent hole, each saved file compared with the original drawn at its own pixels. The gates live in the script.
`--bands 1,7,97,full --heights 600,1000` renders every document through the export entry pressed in bands of each height (`renderPrintAsync`'s `bandRows`; `full` is one band) and exits 1 if any band height prints other bytes than the sheet pressed whole (bands of 1 and 7 only at heights up to 600). `H.render` refuses `bandRows` without `async`: the preview always presses its sheet whole.
The page also holds the save encoders' checks that scripts/check-encode.mjs drives (`H.encodeCheck`, `H.pngCheck`): the JPEG and PNG encoders on a gradient, a saturated halftone and a real print, decoded by the browser.
